.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt PROCESS_MADVISE 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm process_madvise
.Nd advice about the memory of a process a pidfd names
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/mman.h
.In sys/uio.h
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn process_madvise "int pidfd" "const struct iovec *iov" "size_t vlen" "int advice" "unsigned int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn process_madvise
gives the advice
.Fa advice
about each of the
.Fa vlen
ranges of
.Fa iov
in the address space of the process
.Fa pidfd
names, a descriptor of
.Xr pidfd_open 2 ,
as Linux's call does.
Each range is judged as
.Xr madvise 2
judges its own: its base must be page-aligned, its length is rounded up
to a page, and every page of it must be mapped.
The ranges are advised in order, and the first that fails ends the call.
.Pp
The advice is one that discards nothing, as on Linux:
.Dv MADV_WILLNEED
(3),
.Dv MADV_COLD
(20),
.Dv MADV_PAGEOUT
(21) or
.Dv MADV_COLLAPSE
(25).
None changes anything here, there being no swap and no huge pages of user
memory, but
.Dv MADV_COLD
and
.Dv MADV_PAGEOUT
are refused over a locked mapping or a device object's, as
.Xr madvise 2
refuses them.
.Pp
The caller must be allowed to read the process's memory, as
.Xr ptrace 2
allows a read: the process is its own, or the caller is privileged, or
its file system user and group IDs are each of the process's three and the
process is dumpable and not privileged.
Advice about another process's memory needs privilege, Linux's
.Dv CAP_SYS_NICE ,
as well.
.Pa iov
is read in the caller's layout, a
.Vt struct iovec
of two 32-bit words from an i386 program; at most 1024 ranges are taken,
and their lengths are cut so that together they are at most
.Ql 0x7FFFF000 .
.Fa flags
must be 0.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The bytes of the ranges advised.
When the first range fails, or there is none and a check fails, -1 is
returned with
.Va errno
set; the kernel returns the negative error number.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EACCES]"
.It Bq Er EACCES
The caller may not read the process's memory.
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa pidfd
is no pidfd.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
.Fa iov ,
or a range of it, lies outside the caller's memory.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa flags
is not 0,
.Fa vlen
is over 1024, a length is negative as a signed size,
.Fa advice
is not one of the four, a range is not page-aligned or wraps, or the
advice is refused over a mapping of the range.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
A page of a range is not mapped.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The memory is another process's and the caller is not privileged.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
The process has been reaped, or has no address space.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr brk 2 ,
.Xr pidfd_open 2 ,
.Xr process_mrelease 2 ,
.Xr process_vm_readv 2
